Task class: solver-behavior algorithmic. Refines lbd-tiered reducer to match kissat reduce.c semantics.

Background: solver/11-kissat-port/src/main.rs:5413-5421 in reduce_candidate currently gates tier1 (low-LBD) clauses behind emergency mode AND used_recently == 0 AND old age:

    match meta.tier {
        0 => {
            if !emergency
                || meta.used_recently > 0
                || !self.is_old_enough_for_emergency_demote(meta) {
                return None;
            }
        }
        ...
    }

This means tier1 clauses are NEVER candidates outside of emergency reductions, even when their used_recently has aged to 0. Combined with Gap 2 (SAT-playground-qmz, over-budget-only deletion), this lets the tier1 pool grow without bound and compounds the F_full_stack catastrophe seen in analyzesat-2026-05-25.

Kissat (benchmarks/reference-solvers/kissat-latest/src/reduce.c:62-87) treats tier1 differently:

    const unsigned tier1 = TIER1;
    const unsigned tier2 = MAX (tier1, TIER2);
    ...
    if (used) c->used = used - 1;          /* age first */
    if (c->reason) continue;
    const unsigned glue = c->glue;
    if (glue <= tier1 && used) continue;   /* only skip while used > 0 */
    if (glue <= tier2 && used >= MAX_USED - 1) continue;  /* tier2 only skipped while maxed */

So in kissat tier1 clauses ARE candidates once used == 0; they're just at the tail of the
ranking (smaller negative-glue means lower rank). Solver 11 should match: drop the emergency
+ age gate on tier 0 and rely on the candidate ranking + fraction-based deletion (Gap 2) to
keep tier1 clauses around when they remain useful.

Proposed change:

    0 => {
        if meta.used_recently > 0 {
            return None;
        }
    }

This requires Gap 2 (SAT-playground-qmz) to be implemented first so that the reducer does not
delete tier1 clauses by literal-budget pressure alone. Combined, the two changes restore
kissat's mark-then-delete-fraction semantics for all three tiers.

Tests:
* test_reduce_candidate_includes_tier1_when_not_recent
* test_reduce_candidate_skips_tier1_when_recently_used
* test_fraction_deletion_preserves_some_tier1 (verify Gap 2 keeps a meaningful tail of tier1)

Acceptance: cargo test pass, smoke pass; profile bench with SAT_REDUCE=lbd-tiered shows F_full_stack
PAR-2 reduced from 3505 (analyzesat-2026-05-25 A_baseline reference) toward E_combined PAR-2 987.

Evidence: log/analyzesat-2026-05-25-2043/FINDINGS.md (Gap 5 section, code-level recommendation
#4). Companion to SAT-playground-qmz (Gap 2) and SAT-playground-ycw (Gap 3). Closes one of the
two remaining kissat-port reduce-DB gaps identified in this analyzesat run.

Task class: solver-behavior algorithmic. Refines the kissat lbd-tiered port to match kissat's reduce.c deletion semantics.

Background: solver/11-kissat-port/src/main.rs:5165-5224 in reduce_db_lbd_tiered only deletes candidates while learned_literals > learned_lit_budget:

    let mut projected_lits = self.learned_literals;
    self.begin_reduce_delete_marking();
    for idx in 0..self.reduce_candidates.len() {
        let cand = self.reduce_candidates[idx];
        if projected_lits <= self.learned_lit_budget {
            break;   /* stop deleting once under budget */
        }
        self.mark_reduce_delete_candidate(cand.clause_idx);
        projected_lits = projected_lits.saturating_sub(cand.size);
    }

The learned_lit_budget grows as 2000 + 300 * sqrt(reductions) (refresh_learned_lit_budgets, src/main.rs:4402-4421). For instances that produce many small learned clauses, the budget can outgrow the actual learned_literals between reduce passes — the reducer fires but the over-budget gate is never met, so nothing is deleted. Verified on mp1-Nb7T46: D_lbd_tiered ran 71 reduce calls in 240s and averaged ~2k deletions per call (totals from JSON_STATS).

Kissat (benchmarks/reference-solvers/kissat-latest/src/reduce.c:102-151 in mark_less_useful_clauses_as_garbage) always deletes a fixed fraction of candidates per pass:

    const double high = GET_OPTION (reducehigh) * 0.1;   /* 90.0 */
    const double low  = GET_OPTION (reducelow)  * 0.1;   /* 50.0 */
    percent = high - delta / log10(reductions + 9);      /* interpolates 50→90 with reductions */
    target = size * (percent / 100);
    /* delete first target candidates from sorted reducibles */

This means kissat's reducer always does *something*: 50 % of candidates at the start, ramping to 90 % over many reductions. Solver 11's over-budget gate makes the reducer a no-op when learned_literals is small.

Evidence: deeper investigation 2026-05-24 (log/kissat-investigation-2026-05-23-broad/DEEPER_FINDINGS.md, Gap 2). Side-by-side per-instance ablation showed D_lbd_tiered keeps roughly the same learned-clause count as A_default on some instances (mp1 has 266k under D, 425k under A — but A never reduces, vs D firing 71 times). The reducer fires but deletes very few clauses on the structured-SAT failures.

Proposed change: replace the over-budget-gated while-loop in reduce_db_lbd_tiered with a fixed-fraction loop matching kissat's mark_less_useful_clauses_as_garbage. Suggested shape:

    let reductions = self.stats.reduce_db_calls as f64;
    let high = 0.90;
    let low = 0.50;
    let delta = high - low;
    let percent = high - delta / (reductions + 9.0).log10();
    let target = (self.reduce_candidates.len() as f64 * percent) as usize;
    self.begin_reduce_delete_marking();
    for cand in self.reduce_candidates.iter().take(target) {
        self.mark_reduce_delete_candidate(cand.clause_idx);
    }

The over-budget condition can become an emergency-only trigger, separate from the scheduled-reduce default. Keep emergency mode for the hard-budget guard at hard_learned_lit_budget.

Distinction from SAT-playground-5b2.2.44: that bead differentiates tier-2 from tier-1 within the existing over-budget gate (lets tier 2 be evictable on scheduled reductions). This bead is more aggressive: it removes the over-budget gate entirely for scheduled reductions and replaces the all-or-nothing behavior with a kissat-style fixed-fraction policy that operates on all tiers within the keep-rule. The two beads address different aspects of the same issue; they can land together or sequentially.

Implementation notes:
- Replace the while-loop at src/main.rs:5187-5197 with a target-count loop.
- Make reducehigh / reducelow configurable via new SAT_REDUCE_HIGH and SAT_REDUCE_LOW env vars (defaults 90 and 50, matching kissat).
- Verify the sort_unstable_by at src/main.rs:5178-5185 still produces the correct candidate order — kissat sorts by (~size | ~glue << 32), solver 11 sorts by (lbd desc, size desc, used_recently asc, activity_rank asc). Kissat's preferred deletion order is high-glue first, then large-size. Verify solver 11's order matches.
- Update tests for reduce_db_lbd_tiered fraction behavior.

Tests:
  test_reduce_db_deletes_low_fraction_on_first_calls
  test_reduce_db_deletes_high_fraction_after_many_reductions
  test_emergency_over_budget_still_triggers_full_deletion

Acceptance criteria: cargo test pass, smoke pass, invariant smoke pass, profile bench under SAT_USE_LBD=on SAT_REDUCE=lbd-tiered shows expected behavior: smaller learned_clauses_final on instances where the old gate was a no-op (mp1, case9, battleship under D), trajectory unchanged on the wins (sudoku, Kakuro, velev under D). Per +3% promotion rule, target a net PAR-2 improvement on the 10-instance profiling suite under D_lbd_tiered.

Larger project role: closes another of four kissat-port implementation gaps from the 2026-05-24 deep investigation. Should ideally land with the Gap 3 fix (SAT-playground-ycw, mark_learned_clause_recent semantics) since the two together restore kissat's keep-rule + delete-rule balance.

Task class: solver-behavior performance bug. Likely root cause of the SAT_LBD_UPDATE_REASONS / SAT_LBD_UPDATE_PROP_REASONS regressions documented in log/1.14h/summary.md.

Background: src/main.rs:2022-2025 `compute_lbd_for_clause` allocates a `Vec<i32>` on every call:

    fn compute_lbd_for_clause(&mut self, clause_idx: ClauseRef) -> u16 {
        let lits = self.clause_slice(clause_idx).to_vec();
        self.compute_lbd_from_lits(&lits)
    }

The `.to_vec()` exists only to break the borrow conflict between `&self.arena[..]` (for the clause slice) and `&mut self.lbd_seen`/`&mut self.lbd_stamp` inside `compute_lbd_from_lits`. The allocation has no algorithmic purpose.

Call sites:
- src/main.rs:5266-5274 (mark_reason_literals_for_analysis) - hits this once per learned-clause reason during conflict analysis when SAT_LBD_UPDATE_REASONS=on. A wide-UIP conflict touching N learned reasons does N heap allocations + frees of clause-length Vecs.
- src/main.rs:2180 (note_clause_used_as_propagation_reason) - hits this once per propagation that uses a learned clause as reason when SAT_LBD_UPDATE_PROP_REASONS=on. Propagation:conflict ratio is typically 10-100x, so this multiplies the allocation cost.

This is on the hottest path the solver has. The recent profile regression that motivated isolating SAT_LBD_UPDATE_PROP_REASONS behind its own flag (commit 5818f31, "Record feature-mode LBD reason regression") is consistent with this overhead being the dominant cause, not the algorithmic change itself. If the cause is allocation overhead, the feature itself could still be a net win after the fix.

Goal: eliminate the per-call allocation. Two reasonable shapes:

Option 1 (preferred, minimal API churn): add a method that takes the clause slice from caller side and threads &self.decision_level + &mut self.lbd_seen + &mut self.lbd_stamp through a small helper:

    fn compute_lbd_for_clause_idx(&mut self, clause_idx: ClauseRef) -> u16 {
        let len = self.clause_len(clause_idx);
        self.lbd_stamp = self.lbd_stamp.wrapping_add(1);
        if self.lbd_stamp == 0 { self.lbd_seen.fill(0); self.lbd_stamp = 1; }
        let mut count = 0u32;
        for pos in 0..len {
            let lit = self.clause_lit(clause_idx, pos);
            let var = lit.unsigned_abs() as usize;
            let level = *self.decision_level.get(var).unwrap_or(&0);
            if level >= self.lbd_seen.len() { self.lbd_seen.resize(level + 1, 0); }
            if self.lbd_seen[level] != self.lbd_stamp {
                self.lbd_seen[level] = self.lbd_stamp;
                count += 1;
            }
        }
        count.min(u16::MAX as u32) as u16
    }

`clause_len` and `clause_lit` only touch the arena read-only and can coexist with the mutable borrows of the LBD state fields.

Option 2: split solver state so the arena lives in a sub-struct disjoint from `lbd_seen`/`lbd_stamp`; then the existing compute_lbd_from_lits can be called with a true slice borrow. More invasive.

Implementation notes:
- Keep compute_lbd_from_lits for the path that already has a Vec/slice (e.g., learned clause after minimization at src/main.rs:5402).
- Update all call sites that go through compute_lbd_for_clause to use the new non-allocating variant.
- Verify under MIRI or a tracking allocator that the conflict-analysis hot loop stops allocating when SAT_LBD_UPDATE_REASONS=on.

Tests:
- test_compute_lbd_for_clause_matches_compute_lbd_from_lits (random inputs, equality)
- test_compute_lbd_for_clause_no_allocation (tracking allocator under cfg(test))
- existing LBD / conflict-analysis tests must pass unchanged

Benchmark gate: search-core + discriminating, both with SAT_LBD_UPDATE_REASONS=on and with SAT_LBD_UPDATE_REASONS=on SAT_LBD_UPDATE_PROP_REASONS=on. Compare against the baseline-off run AND against the pre-fix on-run. Expected: regression vs. default eliminated or inverted to an improvement. perf stat for instructions / cache-misses around mark_reason_literals_for_analysis and note_clause_used_as_propagation_reason should drop.

Risk IDs: R10 (allocation invariant in hot path), R3 (LBD correctness). Rollback: revert to the cloning helper.

