Task class: solver-behavior / performance-claim subtask of [1.14] Search-throughput pass.

Background: bump_clause_activity in src/main.rs:3239-3258 contains an explicit Vec clone:

    if new_activity > 1e20 {
        let scale = 1e-20;
        let learned_clause_ids = self.learned_clause_ids.clone();
        for learned_clause_idx in learned_clause_ids {
            let scaled = self.clause_activity(learned_clause_idx) * scale;
            self.set_clause_activity(learned_clause_idx, scaled);
        }
        self.clause_activity_inc *= scale;
    }

The clone exists only to satisfy the borrow checker because set_clause_activity needs &mut self while iterating self.learned_clause_ids. For instances with 1M+ learned clauses this is a 4MB+ allocation per rescale. Rescaling is rare per call, but on long UNSAT-heavy runs with many learned clauses it can fire often enough to be visible. It also violates the plan invariant from section 2: "propagation, conflict analysis, decision selection, and reduce-db candidate scans do not allocate in steady state" — bump_clause_activity is reached from conflict-analysis mark_clause_literals_for_analysis (main.rs:4703-4705).

Goal: rescale without cloning. Options to evaluate:
  (a) iterate via index: for i in 0..self.learned_clause_ids.len() { let idx = self.learned_clause_ids[i]; ... }
      The mutation in set_clause_activity touches self.arena, not self.learned_clause_ids, so this should compile.
  (b) extract a helper that takes &mut self.arena directly and the scale, leaving self.learned_clause_ids untouched.
  (c) use std::mem::take to swap the Vec out, iterate the local Vec, swap it back at the end (avoids clone but does two moves of 24 bytes).
Pick whichever is cleanest after a brief experiment; (a) is usually the simplest.

Implementation notes:
- Verify self.set_clause_activity does not transitively touch self.learned_clause_ids.
- The same pattern likely needs auditing in any other clause-activity sweep (clause_activity_decay is just *= so it does not allocate; the rescale path is the only known offender).
- Add an assert / debug counter for the rescale event so we can confirm in benchmarks it actually fires.

Tests:
- test_clause_activity_rescale_preserves_order
- test_clause_activity_rescale_no_allocation (use a tracking allocator in cfg(test))
- test_clause_activity_rescale_correct_inc_scaling
- existing reduce-DB and learned-activity tests must pass unchanged

Benchmark gate: long UNSAT-heavy instances where the rescale is more likely to fire repeatedly (REGRandom K4, SCPC-500, brocard_problem_large). Counter: clause_activity_rescale_events. Expected: identical search trajectory, lower allocator pressure (hot_allocations_estimated if it captures this site), no PAR-2 regression.

